From ba20497bce4961cf9709ce41d94b50a0eecf2acf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <dev@silasmueller.de>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:56:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] pci: vfio-user: register the server's region I/O fds as
 ioeventfds

The vfio-user protocol lets a server name sub-regions of a region whose
accesses the client should deliver through file descriptors instead of
VFIO_USER_REGION_WRITE messages (vfio-user specification, "Region I/O via
file descriptors" and VFIO_USER_DEVICE_GET_REGION_IO_FDS). For an
ioeventfd sub-region the client registers the descriptor with
KVM_IOEVENTFD, so that a guest write there signals the server without an
exit to the VMM. Cloud Hypervisor never asked, so every BAR write of a
vfio-user device took a VM exit and a socket round trip, including
doorbell registers that need no reply.

After mapping a vfio-user device's BARs, ask the server for each BAR's
region I/O fds and register every ioeventfd sub-region that passes the
checks the specification leaves to the client ("it is the client's
responsibility to verify the requested values"):

- type ioeventfd; ioregionfd sub-regions are skipped (KVM has no
  ioregionfd), as are types the specification does not define;
- flags from KVM_IOEVENTFD_FLAG_DATAMATCH and _PIO; _VIRTIO_CCW_NOTIFY
  (s390) is skipped; _PIO must match the BAR type;
- size 0, 1, 2, 4 or 8 (KVM_IOEVENTFD's len); with DATAMATCH a non-zero
  size that holds the datamatch value;
- fd_index within the descriptors received;
- the sub-region inside the BAR, and clear of mmap()ed areas and of the
  MSI-X table and PBA, which the VMM has to keep seeing.

The ioeventfd is registered at the BAR's guest address plus the
sub-region offset, with the length and datamatch as given, moved when
the guest moves the BAR, and unregistered when the device goes away. A
skipped sub-region or a failed registration is logged and the range
stays trapped. A server that answers the request with an error, such as
one that does not implement it, is logged once and the device works as
before.

The hypervisor Vm trait gains register_ioevent_exact() and
unregister_ioevent_exact(), which pass KVM_IOEVENTFD's len and datamatch
through. register_ioevent() ties the length to the datamatch type (0, 4
or 8 bytes) and unregister_ioevent() only removes an ioeventfd without
datamatch. KVM implements the new calls; other hypervisors refuse them,
which leaves the ranges trapped.

The request and the reply's wire format are the vfio_user crate's.
Client::get_region_io_fds() is in no release yet (proposed to
rust-vmm/vfio), so the workspace takes vfio_user from a path beside this
tree through [patch.crates-io]: 0.1.6 with the change, locked to the
vm-memory 0.17.1 and vfio-bindings 0.6.2 the rest of the tree uses.

The crate accepts a reply only in the specification's layout (40-byte
entries, datamatch at offset 32, types ioeventfd and ioregionfd). A reply
in another form, such as libvfio-user's 48-byte ioeventfd entries with
datamatch at offset 40, is logged and leaves that region trapped; no
ioeventfd is registered from it.

Tests: the sub-region checks, accepted and refused cases; a reply built
byte by byte in the specification's layout, and one in libvfio-user's,
through the crate's parser.

